{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Interpreted the over-subscription revision, which refutes the occupancy explanation rather than the defect. With 320 concurrent racing calls against 64 verifyCh slots, the post-shutdown strand persisted: 6, 5 and 6 of eight calls answered and 2, 3 and 2 left incomplete, versus 5, 1 and 3 answered with 3, 7 and 5 stranded in the 128-call cases from the same run, so the declared prediction that over-subscribing would answer every later call was contradicted with three valid witnesses. The reading recorded is that the surplus senders are released by the shutdown case instead of holding slots, leaving the buffer partially empty at the exit so later callers still find a free slot; occupancy is therefore not what decides the strand. I added that as counterevidence, corrected the limitation that described the buffer as never filling, extended the Fact under its own id with both receipts' indices and the removed explanation, and extended the joint history to cover the fifth revision. The defect claim, the earlier counts and the unbuffered and BatchApplyCh contrasts are unchanged, and no execution is requested.\"}"}}
